{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RegTraceGramProduct","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RegTraceGramProduct","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RegTraceMatrix","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Block","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.StdBasis"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_matrix_det_ne_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_matrix_det_ne_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":195,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L195-L208","doc":"**The product Gram determinant vanishes exactly when some block dimension dies in `K`.**\n\nThe sharp characteristic statement for a semisimple algebra in Wedderburn form: the regular\ntrace form degenerates iff `char K` divides one of the block dimensions.  Over a\ncharacteristic-zero field no block can die, which is the route\n`RegTraceGramNondegenerate` takes to `det(twGram) ≠ 0` — this says the char-zero hypothesis\nthere is not merely sufficient but exactly right. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_matrix_det","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_matrix_det","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L178-L193","doc":"**The Gram determinant of a product of matrix algebras**, in the product of matrix-unit\nbases: the product over blocks of `± d_i^(d_i²)`.\n\nThis is `regTraceGram_pi_det'` composed with `regTraceGram_stdBasis_det`, and it is the\nderivation of the integer constant that\n`computations/hecke/tw_gram_determinant_probe.py` measured by factoring exact symbolic\ndeterminants.  Up to the signs it is `∏_i d_i^(d_i²)`; reading the block dimensions off the\nWedderburn decomposition of `H_{n+1}` — the SYT counts of the partitions of `n+1` — that is\n`1`, `2⁴`, `2⁴·3¹⁸` at `n = 1, 2, 3`, the probe's three measured constants. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_det'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_det'","kind":"theorem","line":164,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L164-L171","doc":"`regTraceGram_pi_det` with no order hypothesis: any `Fintype` admits a linear order, and\nthe conclusion does not mention it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_det","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_det","kind":"theorem","line":153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L153-L162","doc":"**The assembly**: the Gram determinant of a finite product of algebras, in a product\nbasis, is the product of the factors' Gram determinants.\n\nThe linear order on `ι` is inherited from `BlockTriangular.det_fintype` and is invisible in\nthe statement; `regTraceGram_pi_det'` supplies one for an arbitrary index type. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_diagonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_diagonal","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L143-L151","doc":"**The diagonal blocks are the factors' own Gram matrices.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_offDiagonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceGram_pi_offDiagonal","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L136-L141","doc":"**The Gram matrix of the product, in a product basis, is block diagonal.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceForm_pi_single_same","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceForm_pi_single_same","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L123-L132","doc":"**Same factor**: the form restricts to the factor's own regular trace form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceForm_pi_single_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.regTraceForm_pi_single_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L109-L121","doc":"**Different factors of a product algebra are orthogonal for the regular trace form.**\n\nEvery summand of `regTraceForm_pi` is killed: at index `i ≠ j` the first argument is zero,\nand at `i = j ≠ k` the second is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.det_blockDiagonal'","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.det_blockDiagonal'","kind":"theorem","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L82-L98","doc":"**`Matrix.det_blockDiagonal'` — the named Mathlib gap, as an immediate corollary.**\n\nMathlib has `Matrix.det_blockDiagonal` for `blockDiagonal` (uniform block size) and no\ndeterminant lemma at all for `blockDiagonal'` (dependent block sizes).  This supplies it, and\nthe point of stating it here is evidential: it shows that\n`det_eq_prod_of_offDiagonal_eq_zero` really is the general form of the missing lemma, and\nthat the missing lemma was never the obstruction — the whole proof is\n`BlockTriangular.det_fintype` plus a fibre reindex. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.det_eq_prod_of_offDiagonal_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.det_eq_prod_of_offDiagonal_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RegTraceGramProduct.lean#L64-L80","doc":"**A block-diagonal matrix over a sigma index has determinant the product of its blocks**,\nwith the blocks allowed to have *different* sizes.\n\nThis is the general form of what `Matrix.det_blockDiagonal` gives only for uniform blocks.\nThe whole content is `Matrix.BlockTriangular.det_fintype` plus the identification of the\nfibre `{p // p.1 = k}` with `κ k`; the linear order on `ι` is an artifact of that lemma and\ndoes not appear in the conclusion. ","signatureChunk":0}]}